A.cards("c8s1",[{"id": "g8-1-standard", "h": "8.1 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-1-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.1"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Force, stress and pressure</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A force is a push or pull. Stress is force divided by the area over which it acts, so the same force on a smaller area produces greater stress. Confining pressure acts equally from every direction; differential stress differs with direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform without confusing the response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock may translate to a new position, rotate to a new orientation, or change its shape and size. That internal change is strain. A tilted bed records rotation; flattened pebbles record internal strain. More than one response can occur together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow strength into the crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On the supplied strength graph, depth increases downward and strength increases to the right. In shallow brittle rock, confinement generally increases resistance to breaking. Deeper, hotter rock can deform by solid-state flow and strength decreases. The curve peaks near the transition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the limits visible</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The brittle–ductile transition is a change in dominant behavior, not a universal fixed depth or a melting line. Temperature, composition, fluids, pressure, and strain rate affect how a rock responds. The graph is a model, not a single depth that applies everywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Equal loading from all directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Confining pressure</mark>.</li><li>Unequal stresses in different directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Differential stress</mark>.</li><li>Rock moved to a new position → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Displacement</mark>.</li><li>Originally horizontal bed tilted → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rotation</mark>.</li><li>Pebble changed shape internally → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strain</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Force, stress and pressure · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Deform without confusing the response · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow strength into the crust · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Keep the limits visible · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
