A.cards("c8s2",[{"id": "g8-2-standard", "h": "8.2 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-2-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.2"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compression, tension, and shear</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression squeezes and shortens rock, tension stretches it, and shear makes adjacent material move past itself. The same stress category can produce different structures under different conditions. In the shallow crust, compression can form faults; deeper, hot rocks can fold or flow.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From brittle fracture to solid flow</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A brittle rock breaks or slips when stressed. At depth, higher confinement and temperature commonly favor ductile behavior: minerals and rock masses change shape without a throughgoing fracture. Ductile does not mean liquid, and the transition depends on the material and conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow minerals and fluids</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At increasing depth, minerals may deform, grow, or react to form new minerals stable under changed conditions. Water in pore spaces opposes part of the confining pressure and can assist fracture or reactions. Fluid effects depend on location and conditions rather than one universal outcome.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use experiments and observations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Geologists investigate mineral stability and rock behavior using controlled temperature and pressure experiments, field observations, and measurements of natural rocks. Each line of evidence has a defined range of conditions. A single specimen cannot establish the strength of every rock at a given depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Squeezing that shortens rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Compression</mark>.</li><li>Pulling that stretches rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension</mark>.</li><li>Adjacent material moves past itself → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shear</mark>.</li><li>Fracture and slip near the surface → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Brittle behavior</mark>.</li><li>Solid rock changes shape without a throughgoing break → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Ductile behavior</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compression, tension, and shear · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"From brittle fracture to solid flow · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow minerals and fluids · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use experiments and observations · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
