A.cards("c8s3",[{"id": "g8-3-standard", "h": "8.3 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-3-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.3"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish two fractures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A joint is a crack with little or no shear displacement along its surface. A fault is a fracture across which rocks have slipped. Follow a matching bed or other marker across the break before deciding whether movement occurred.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Open joints under different causes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joints can open as rocks cool and contract, as overlying rock is removed and pressure decreases, or under tectonic stresses. Columnar joints form during cooling. Sheet-like unloading joints follow reduced confinement as deeply formed rock approaches the surface.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relate joint orientation to stress</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An opening-mode joint is oriented approximately perpendicular to the direction in which the rock is pulled apart. Joint sets can divide an outcrop into regular blocks. Identify the opening direction and the visible planes before deciding which stresses acted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Faults cut and offset</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Differential stress can produce a fracture along which blocks slip. The fault orientation depends on the full stress field, including stresses in the other directions. An observed fracture alone does not establish the amount or sense of motion without a displaced marker or other evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Open fracture with little shear offset → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint</mark>.</li><li>Fracture with relative slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault</mark>.</li><li>Contraction during cooling → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Columnar joints</mark>.</li><li>Reduced pressure during erosion and uplift → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Unloading joints</mark>.</li><li>Open crack roughly perpendicular to pull → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension joint</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Distinguish two fractures · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" alt=\"Open joints under different causes · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Relate joint orientation to stress · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Faults cut and offset · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
