A.cards("c8s4",[{"id": "g8-4-standard", "h": "8.4 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-4-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.4"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Orient a planar surface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Strike is the direction of a horizontal line on an inclined plane. Dip is the direction and angle of steepest descent of that plane. Slip may be along dip, along strike, or oblique. Plane orientation and movement direction answer different questions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the two walls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For an inclined fault, the hanging wall is the block above the plane and the footwall lies below it. The labels follow the fault plane; they are not assigned by which block appears higher on the modern landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare fault movements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On a normal fault the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall, commonly during extension. On a reverse fault it moves up, commonly during shortening; a thrust is a low-angle reverse fault. Strike-slip faults have predominantly horizontal movement.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build horsts and grabens</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A graben is a block that has dropped relative to blocks on either side along normal faults. A horst is relatively raised. These are relative displacements within a fault system, so present elevation alone does not define every block’s complete history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Block above an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hanging wall</mark>.</li><li>Block below an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Footwall</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves down → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normal fault</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves up → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Reverse fault</mark>.</li><li>Predominantly horizontal slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strike-slip fault</mark>.</li><li>Dropped block between normal faults → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graben</mark>.</li><li>Relatively raised block → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Horst</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Orient a planar surface · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Identify the two walls · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Compare fault movements · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Build horsts and grabens · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
