A.cards("c8s5",[{"id": "g8-5-standard", "h": "8.5 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-5-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.5"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shorten layered rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can shorten and bend layered rocks into folds while the layers remain traceable. The curved region of maximum bending is the hinge, and the sides are limbs. Trace the same layer through the whole structure before naming a fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare anticlines and synclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An anticline has older rocks in its core and a syncline has younger rocks in its core. In an upright fold, anticlines commonly arch upward and synclines trough downward. Overturning complicates simple shape rules, so use age relations where they are known.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Domes, basins, and monoclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A dome is a roughly circular structure with older rocks in its center; a basin has younger rocks in its center. A monocline is a step-like bend connecting more gently dipping segments. Modern topography can differ because rocks erode at different rates.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe axial orientation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An axial surface joins hinges through successive layers. It can be upright or inclined. A fold is overturned where a limb has rotated beyond vertical. A plunging hinge is inclined rather than horizontal. These descriptions address different aspects of geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Older rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Anticline</mark>.</li><li>Younger rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Step-like bend in otherwise gently dipping layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Monocline</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with older central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dome</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with younger central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Basin</mark>.</li><li>Surface connecting successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>Two limbs dip by different amounts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Asymmetric fold</mark>.</li><li>A hinge is inclined rather than horizontal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Plunging fold</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Shorten layered rock · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compare anticlines and synclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Domes, basins, and monoclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Describe axial orientation · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
