A.cards("c8s6",[{"id": "g8-6-standard", "h": "8.6 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-6-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.6"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock cleavage is a tendency to split along closely spaced surfaces produced by deformation and mineral alignment. It may cut across the original bedding. Slate is a fine-grained rock that commonly has well-developed cleavage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize foliation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation is a planar fabric expressed by aligned minerals or compositional layers. Some rocks split into thin sheets, while others show coarse light and dark bands. Distinguish this metamorphic fabric from depositional bedding that may still be visible.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize lineation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lineation is a linear fabric: elongated minerals, stretched objects, or intersections of planar features align along a direction. It is not the same geometric feature as a planar foliation. Different lineations reflect different processes and should be described before interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read inherited structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relict bedding, clasts, and other features can survive metamorphism and record the parent rock. Minerals can rotate, grow, and become elongated during deformation. Compare these younger fabrics with preserved older structures rather than assuming all lines are bedding.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Closely spaced splitting planes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Planar alignment or mineral banding → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Aligned elongated objects → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lineation</mark>.</li><li>Sedimentary layer preserved through metamorphism → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Relict bedding</mark>.</li><li>A metamorphic plane cuts an older sedimentary layer → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage across bedding</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize cleavage · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize foliation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize lineation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read inherited structures · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
