A.cards("c8s7",[{"id": "g8-7-standard", "h": "8.7 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-7-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.7"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow a shale-derived sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For suitable shale-rich parent material, increasing metamorphic grade can produce slate, then phyllite, schist, and gneiss. Slate is dull and fine grained, phyllite has a sheen, schist has visible aligned minerals, and gneiss commonly displays compositional bands.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform sandstone and limestone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Quartz-rich sandstone can become quartzite as quartz grains recrystallize and interlock; fractures may cut across grains. Limestone can become marble, a rock of recrystallized carbonate minerals. Both can have weak foliation, so use composition and texture together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform igneous parent rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Mafic volcanic rocks can produce greenstone or other metamorphic rocks as mineral assemblages change. Felsic volcanic and plutonic rocks have different compositions and can form different products. A metamorphic name depends on the rock and its observable features, not grade alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use multiple clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hornfels commonly forms when rock is heated near an intrusion. Gneissic banding can form in several parent compositions, and the parent of a particular gneiss may remain uncertain. Mineralogy, grain size, fabric, and field context jointly support identification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine dull rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Fine rock with a silky sheen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Phyllite</mark>.</li><li>Visible aligned mica-rich minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Schist</mark>.</li><li>Coarse light and dark mineral bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Interlocking quartz grains derived from sandstone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Recrystallized carbonate derived from limestone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Contact-heated, fine-grained rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hornfels</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Follow a shale-derived sequence · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform sandstone and limestone · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform igneous parent rocks · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use multiple clues · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
