A.cards("c8s8",[{"id": "g8-8-standard", "h": "8.8 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-8-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.8"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Conditions drive solid-state change</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heat, pressure, differential stress, and chemically active fluids can change rock minerals or texture. Burial, tectonic activity, nearby magma, and fluid circulation provide different combinations of those drivers. Metamorphism and deformation may occur together or independently.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change grain size or mineral identity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Recrystallization can enlarge existing mineral crystals. Neocrystallization forms new minerals as reactions make a different assemblage stable. These terms describe different changes; neither means the whole rock must melt and freeze again.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dissolve, transport, and rearrange</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Material can dissolve at stressed grain contacts and move in fluids. Chemical components can be redistributed, producing different mineral-rich layers. Whether the bulk composition changes depends on movement into or out of the rock, which must be supported by evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform mineral fabrics</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During deformation, minerals and objects can rotate, flatten, stretch, or form aligned fabrics. Metamorphism can preserve older features as relicts while adding new minerals and foliation. Read the sequence of overprinting rather than assuming all features formed simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Existing grains grow into larger crystals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Recrystallization</mark>.</li><li>New mineral species grow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Neocrystallization</mark>.</li><li>Material dissolves at stressed contacts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pressure solution</mark>.</li><li>Minerals align into a planar fabric → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Minerals move into different bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chemical remobilization</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Conditions drive solid-state change · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Change grain size or mineral identity · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Dissolve, transport, and rearrange · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Deform mineral fabrics · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
