A.cards("c8s9",[{"id": "g8-9-standard", "h": "8.9 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-9-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.9"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read pressure and temperature separately</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied diagram distinguishes high-temperature, low-pressure conditions; high-pressure, relatively low-temperature conditions; and typical regional conditions. Pressure increases with burial, but temperature depends on the thermal history. High pressure does not always mean the hottest rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Heat beside magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A shallow intrusion heats adjacent country rock and creates a contact aureole. That setting can reach high temperature at relatively low pressure. The aureole’s extent and mineral assemblages depend on heat, host composition, and fluids.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduct a relatively cold slab</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At an ocean–continent convergent boundary, a relatively cold slab descends and experiences increasing pressure before it fully warms. High-pressure, low-temperature metamorphism can occur along the subduction zone; a shallow magma body has a contrasting pressure–temperature history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bury rocks during collision</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During continent–continent collision, thrusting and crustal thickening bury and heat rocks over broad regions. Regional metamorphism and deformation can accompany one another. Map the location and conditions separately rather than assigning one condition to an entire plate boundary.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shallow rock heated near an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High temperature, low pressure</mark>.</li><li>Relatively cold material carried deep in subduction → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High pressure, low temperature</mark>.</li><li>Broad burial and heating in a mountain belt → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regional metamorphism</mark>.</li><li>Zone heated around an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact aureole</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read pressure and temperature separately · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Subduct a relatively cold slab · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Bury rocks during collision · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
