A.cards("c8s11",[{"id": "g8-11-standard", "h": "8.11 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-11-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.11"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Link deformation structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults, joints, folds, tilted layers, and shear zones can form in related systems. A fault propagating upward can bend layers above it, and slip over a bend in a thrust fault can fold the overlying rocks. Structural associations must be supported by geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a fold-and-thrust belt</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can move thrust sheets over one another and shorten a region. Beds in and above the sheets may form anticlines and synclines. Related joints, smaller faults, and metamorphic fabrics can occur, but not every nearby structure must share the same age.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare bedding with cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cleavage in a fold commonly lies roughly parallel to the axial surface. It cuts bedding at different angles on the limbs and may be nearly perpendicular to bedding near a hinge in the supplied example. Trace both surfaces across the fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate shared from later events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A younger fault can cut an older fold, and an older fault can itself be folded. Use cross-cutting, overprinting, and the geometry of displaced markers to order events. Proximity alone does not prove two structures formed together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fault bends overlying layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-related fold</mark>.</li><li>Low-angle reverse faults stack sheets → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fold-and-thrust belt</mark>.</li><li>Metamorphic splitting crosses beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Surface through successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>A later structure cuts an earlier one → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross-cutting relation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Link deformation structures · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Build a fold-and-thrust belt · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare bedding with cleavage · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Separate shared from later events · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
