A.cards("c8s12",[{"id": "g8-12-standard", "h": "8.12 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-12-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.12"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Joints guide erosion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joint sets divide rock into blocks and provide pathways along which weathering can act. Their orientation can influence cliffs, pillars, notches, and drainage. Recognize the fracture patterns in an outcrop before attributing them to one particular stress history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Find a fault in the terrain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults can produce aligned notches, scarps, crushed angular rock, displaced layers, and striations. Several observations are more persuasive than a straight valley alone. A scarp shows relative topographic offset but does not by itself identify the complete fault history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode tilted and folded layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Resistant tilted beds can support ridges and dip slopes while weaker units erode into lower ground. A ridge does not automatically mark an anticline, and a valley does not automatically mark a syncline. Follow rock layers and ages through the landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read metamorphic outcrops</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation, cleavage, and resistant folded layers can influence the shapes of metamorphic outcrops. Modern topography is a product of both structure and erosion. Distinguish a visible rock fabric from a hypothesis about when it formed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Repeated fractures divide rock into blocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint sets</mark>.</li><li>Crushed rock and offset marker beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li><li>Slope approximately follows an inclined bed → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dip slope</mark>.</li><li>Resistant tilted bed forms a narrow ridge → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hogback</mark>.</li><li>Planar mineral alignment in outcrop → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Joints guide erosion · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Find a fault in the terrain · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Erode tilted and folded layers · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read metamorphic outcrops · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
