A.cards("c8s14",[{"id": "g8-14-standard", "h": "8.14 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-14-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.14"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the regional map</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>New England contains different rock belts, faults, folds, and metamorphic rocks. The supplied map and cross section show structures at the surface and interpreted relations below it. Use the legend and orientation before comparing locations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate collision from later rifting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic collisions contributed to Appalachian deformation and metamorphism. Later Mesozoic extension opened rift basins as North America separated from Africa. These are separate events; a modern map can display structures inherited from both.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare metamorphic grade</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source map shows the highest metamorphic conditions in the central part of its illustrated area, decreasing toward both east and west. Grade is constrained using mineral assemblages and textures, not topographic elevation alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied regional interpretation, rocks in eastern Vermont were buried and then heated, while rocks in New Hampshire were initially heated and then buried. These paths are evidence-based reconstructions for specific locations, not a universal order for all mountain belts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic assembly of Appalachian mountains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Continental collision</mark>.</li><li>Younger Mesozoic extension → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rifting</mark>.</li><li>Highest grade in the supplied regional map → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Central part of the area</mark>.</li><li>Eastern Vermont path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Burial followed by heating</mark>.</li><li>New Hampshire path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Heating followed by burial</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read the regional map · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Separate collision from later rifting · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare metamorphic grade · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
