A.cards("c8s15",[{"id": "g8-15-standard", "h": "8.15 · Mechanisms and evidence", "c": ["c8-15-core"], "src": ["TB8:8.15"], "html": "<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the terrain and fold</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied north-looking perspective, blocky hills have bedding dip slopes facing toward the center. The cross section shows a down-curving fold, and near-vertical cleavage cuts bedding. The observations support an upright or steeply inclined syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the rock samples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The eastern coarse black-and-white banded sample is gneiss; the dull fine-grained sample with cleavage is slate. Interlocking quartz grains support quartzite, while fine calcite and deformed fossils support marble. Quartzite commonly began as sandstone and marble as limestone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the two sides of the stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A straight stream follows an intensely fractured and shattered zone between the eastern banded rocks and lower-grade rocks to the west. The observations suggest a fault zone; the checked Connect summary calls the boundary a reverse fault. Identify that key as supplied assessment evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer shortening and history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source perspective looks north, and the fold and cleavage indicate east–west shortening in the supplied checked summary. The eastern banded rocks are higher grade than the rocks in the grassy area and southern hill. Separate those source-specific relations from rules for every syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coarse black-and-white bands with visible minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Dull fine-grained rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Hard interlocking quartz grains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Fine calcite with deformed fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Down-curving layers with inward-dipping limbs → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Straight stream through intensely shattered rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Observe the terrain and fold · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Identify the rock samples · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Infer shortening and history · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>", "examId": "exam2", "traps": ["Use the observed geometry, rock properties and source-specific conditions before deciding on a process or history."], "media": []}]);
